/* Sonnet-written notes explaining each connection. Generated by scripts/explain-connections.mjs. */
window.ATLAS_CONN_NOTES = {"m:lb-tripoli-international-stadium|lm-7-october-stadium|context":{"headline":"Before the June 11 Stadium was built, the Municipal Stadium (now 7 October) was Tripoli's only football stadium, according to Wikipedia.","explanation":"The 7 October Stadium was built in 1939 and was first called the Municipal Stadium. One Wikipedia article says it was the only football stadium in Tripoli until the June 11 Stadium was built. That makes the older ground the city's football home before the newer, larger one took over. The recorded year of 1970 is not confirmed, because the sources disagree on when the June 11 Stadium was built. The article says \"the 1970s\", and a Wikipedia timeline says 1982.","strength":"reported","flag":"ok","established":["The 7 October Stadium was built in 1939 and was first called the Municipal Stadium (Docs 1 and 2).","Doc 1 says it was Tripoli's only football stadium before the June 11 Stadium was built.","Doc 1 says it is now a training ground for Al-Ittihad Club."],"not_established":["The year 1970 is not confirmed. Doc 1 says only 'the 1970s', and Doc 2 lists the June 11 Stadium opening in 1982.","The sources do not say how the Municipal Stadium was used under Gaddafi, or when it was renamed 7 October.","Both are Wikipedia pages, with no original records or eyewitness accounts behind them."],"context":"The June 11 Stadium is named after the date in 1970 when US forces left Wheelus base. The name does not by itself show when the stadium opened.","timeline":[{"when":"1939","what":"Municipal Stadium (now 7 October) built in Tripoli"},{"when":"1970s","what":"June 11 Stadium built, according to the 7 October Stadium article"},{"when":"1982","what":"The Tripoli timeline lists the June 11 Stadium as opening"}],"quotes":[{"text":"It was named Municipal Stadium and was the only football stadium in Tripoli, Libya before the June 11 Stadium was built in the 1970s.","doc":{"id":"wiki-en-7 October Stadium","t":"7 October Stadium (Wikipedia)"}}],"open_question":"When did the June 11 Stadium actually open, 1970 or 1982? Libyan sports ministry records, or press from the time, would settle it."},"m:lb-british-embassy-tripoli|lb-italian-embassy-tripoli|event":{"headline":"The British and Italian embassies in Tripoli are linked because both were reportedly burned by mobs on 1 May 2011.","explanation":"The family's record says mobs burned both embassies on 1 May 2011, after a NATO strike on Gaddafi's compound. The only source cited is an Atlantic Council blog post whose title matches that claim. No passage from it could be retrieved, so this note rests on the recorded reason and the title alone. The link matters because it shows foreign missions in Tripoli were attacked in the final months of Gaddafi's rule. The British embassy's own profile gives the same date and cause. The map points may not mark the buildings that burned.","strength":"reported","flag":"ok","established":["The family's record states that mobs burned both embassies on 1 May 2011 after a NATO strike.","The cited Atlantic Council post is titled 'Libyans burn British and Italian embassies after NATO strike'.","The British embassy profile separately says a mob burned the embassy and ambassador's residence on 1 May 2011, leaving 'burned shells'."],"not_established":["No text from the cited source was available, so details such as who the mobs were, the damage to the Italian embassy, and the target of the strike are unchecked.","It is not confirmed that the mapped locations are the buildings that burned; both profiles say they may not be."],"context":"NATO was carrying out air operations over Libya in 2011 during the uprising against Gaddafi. Reports at the time described the attacks as following a strike in which one of Gaddafi's sons was said to have been killed.","timeline":[{"when":"1 May 2011","what":"NATO strike on Gaddafi's compound, then mobs burn the British and Italian embassies (as recorded)."}],"quotes":[],"open_question":"The text of the Atlantic Council post, or contemporary news reports and diplomatic statements, would confirm the damage to each embassy and show where the buildings stood."},"m:hadba|ainzara|transfer":{"headline":"A 2017 record says 77 men tied to the 1996 Abu Salim killings were held at al-Hadba, then confirmed at Ain Zara. We could not check it.","explanation":"The link is a transfer of detainees between two Tripoli prisons in 2017, six years after Gaddafi's rule ended. The recorded reason says 77 detainees linked to the 1996 Abu Salim killings were held at al-Hadba on 17 May 2017. It says they were later confirmed at Ain Zara in November 2017. The cited source is a 2018 UN report, \"Abuse Behind Bars\". No passage from it was available to us, so we have only the stated reason. The link matters because it ties two sites to the aftermath of an event from Gaddafi's time. It does not show that either site was used for detention before 2011. The report seems to say the men were \"confirmed\" at Ain Zara in November. That is not the same as a recorded transfer date or a stated move between the two sites.","strength":"reported","flag":"ok","established":["The recorded reason states that 77 detainees linked to the 1996 Abu Salim killings were at al-Hadba on 17 May 2017.","The same reason states they were later confirmed at Ain Zara in November 2017.","The cited source is the UN OHCHR/UNSMIL 2018 report \"Abuse Behind Bars\"."],"not_established":["We did not see the source text, so the figures and dates are unchecked.","The record does not say whether the men were moved directly between the two sites, or when or how.","Nothing here shows either site held detainees before 2011."],"context":"The Abu Salim killings of 1996 refer to the deaths of many prisoners at Abu Salim prison in Tripoli. Cases linked to them were still unresolved long after 2011.","timeline":[{"when":"1996","what":"Abu Salim prison killings, the event the detainees are linked to."},{"when":"17 May 2017","what":"77 detainees recorded as held at al-Hadba."},{"when":"November 2017","what":"The same group confirmed at Ain Zara."},{"when":"2018","what":"UN OHCHR/UNSMIL publishes \"Abuse Behind Bars\"."}],"quotes":[],"open_question":"The full text of the UN report, \"Abuse Behind Bars\" (2018), would show exactly what it says about the 77 men, the two dates and any move between the sites."},"u:dougia|n'djamena":{"headline":"A December 1980 U.S. imagery report names Dougia and N'Djamena together as places where Libya increased its military presence in Chad.","explanation":"The report lists Dougia and Ndjamena (the Chadian capital) among the areas where Libyan forces grew in 1980. That is the only link the passage supports. It does not say what was at Dougia or what its connection to N'Djamena was. The summary says new airfields were reported at Aozou and Ndjamena, and that Libyan aircraft and ground forces were seen at several Chadian airfields. The passage shown does not call Dougia an airfield. The link is that both were part of one Libyan deployment in Chad. It says nothing directly about Tripoli, though it shows how far Libya's military reach extended from there.","strength":"weak","flag":"ok","established":["The report says Libya increased its presence in Chad significantly in 1980, especially around Ndjamena, Dougia, Faya Largeau, Fada, Oum Chalouba, Ounianga Kebir and Aozou.","Ndjamena is described as the capital of Chad.","The two places are named together in the same sentence of one report."],"not_established":["The passage does not say Dougia has an airfield, so the AI's reason that both are airfields is not shown by the text given.","It does not show any direct movement, contact or relationship between Dougia and N'Djamena.","The summary mentions an airfield at Ndjamena, but the passage shown does not describe it."],"context":"Libya intervened in Chad's civil war in 1980, backing President Goukouni Oueddei. Libyan forces were in Chad for much of the following decade.","timeline":[{"when":"Late June 1980","what":"An airfield is identified under construction north-northeast of Aozou."},{"when":"1980","what":"Libya increases its military presence in Chad, including at Ndjamena and Dougia."},{"when":"December 1980","what":"The imagery report on Libyan activity in Chad is issued."}],"quotes":[{"text":"During this period the Libyans increased their presence in Chad significantly, especially in the areas of Ndjamena, the capital, and Dougia, Faya Largeau, Fada","doc":{"id":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp81t00034r000100160001-9","t":"LIBYAN MILITARY ACTIVITY IN CHAD (SANITIZED)"}}],"open_question":"The full report, including its figures and any airfield tables, would show whether Dougia had an airfield and what Libya had there compared with N'Djamena."},"m:lh-al-waddan-hotel|lm-radisson-blu-al-mahary|same-place":{"headline":"Two different Tripoli hotels, the Uaddan and the Mehari, were built in 1935 by the same architect, so \"same-place\" looks wrong.","explanation":"The recorded reason says the Uaddan Hotel and the Hotel del Mehari (now the Radisson) were built together in 1935 by Florestano Di Fausto. That describes two separate hotels with a shared architect and date, not one place under two names. The link is better described as \"related buildings\" than \"same-place\". No source passage could be retrieved, so this note relies only on the stated reason and the two atlas profiles. The profiles agree that both hotels were designed by Di Fausto with Stefano Gatti-Casazza. They describe the Uaddan as having a casino and theatre, and the Mehari as rebuilt in 1989 and 2009. Neither profile says how the hotels were used under Gaddafi.","strength":"inferred","flag":"unsupported","established":["The atlas profiles say both hotels were Italian-era buildings from 1935, designed by Florestano Di Fausto with Stefano Gatti-Casazza.","The Uaddan profile says it was built alongside the Hotel Al Mehari.","The two hotels are described as separate buildings with different histories."],"not_established":["No source passage was available to check the claim directly.","The sources do not show that the two hotels are the same place or on the same site.","The sources do not say how either hotel was used under Gaddafi."],"context":"Under Italian rule in the 1930s, Tripoli's waterfront saw several new public buildings. Hotels were part of that building programme.","timeline":[{"when":"1935","what":"Both hotels built to designs by Florestano Di Fausto, according to the profiles."},{"when":"1936","what":"Uaddan Hotel and Casino opened, according to the profile text."},{"when":"1989","what":"The present Mehari/Mahary hotel opened."},{"when":"2009","what":"Mahary fully remodelled under the Radisson name."}],"quotes":[],"open_question":"A map or plan of 1930s Tripoli, or the two Wikipedia articles' own text, would show whether the hotels stood side by side as separate buildings. Recheck the \"same-place\" label against that."},"u:egypt|libya foreign relations":{"headline":"In a September 1976 Tripoli speech, Qadhdhafi said he would have cut all ties with Egypt, but that the decision belonged to the Libyan people.","explanation":"The one source is a transcript of a Qadhdhafi rally speech broadcast from Tripoli on 2 September 1976. In it he says decisions on Libya's relations with other countries now rest with the people, not a president or government. He then says that if the choice had been his, he would not have hesitated to sever all relations with Egypt. He gives Egypt's alliance with the United States and Israel as one reason. The speech shows Libya's open hostility toward Egypt and how Qadhdhafi described the way foreign policy was made. It does not show that relations were actually cut.","strength":"direct","flag":"ok","established":["Qadhdhafi said that, had the decision been his, he would have severed all relations with the Egyptian government.","He accused Egypt of acting in alliance with the United States and Israel against the Libyan people.","He said decisions on foreign relations are now made by the people, not by a president or government.","The speech was broadcast from Tripoli and recorded on 2 September 1976."],"not_established":["The passage does not show that Libya actually broke relations with Egypt.","The last 50 minutes of the speech are marked indistinct, so the fuller argument is missing.","The transcript does not say what Egyptian actions he was referring to beyond the accusations quoted."],"context":"Libya's relations with Egypt were strained in the mid-1970s, after Egypt moved closer to the United States and toward dealings with Israel. Qadhdhafi described Libya's system as rule by popular congresses.","timeline":[{"when":"2 Sep 1976","what":"Qadhdhafi gives a rally speech, broadcast from Tripoli, criticising Egypt."}],"quotes":[],"open_question":"Later broadcasts or diplomatic records from 1976 could show whether Libya formally cut or changed its ties with Egypt after this speech."},"u:map:fateh|moussa koussa":{"headline":"Moussa Koussa, later intelligence chief and foreign minister, is said to have taught at the University of Tripoli for decades.","explanation":"One source, a Wikipedia biography, says Koussa taught at the University of Tripoli for decades. It says he kept teaching even while serving the Libyan government in other roles. It gives this after describing his American studies and return to Libya. This shows that a senior security figure also had a place in Tripoli's academic life. The source gives no dates, subject or details of his teaching. It also does not say whether the university is the same one later called Al-Fateh University, though that is widely known to be the case.","strength":"reported","flag":"ok","established":["Wikipedia states that Koussa taught at the University of Tripoli for decades.","It says he did this even while holding government posts."],"not_established":["No years, subject or position held at the university are given.","The passage does not cite a source for the teaching claim, and no second source confirms it.","It does not link the university to his intelligence work."],"context":"The University of Tripoli was renamed Al-Fateh University under Gaddafi and later took its older name back. The passage's name for it is the University of Tripoli.","timeline":[{"when":"After his return to Libya","what":"Koussa gave up plans for a doctorate and came back to Tripoli."},{"when":"Over several decades","what":"He is said to have taught at the University of Tripoli while serving in government."}],"quotes":[{"text":"He taught at the University of Tripoli for decades, even while serving the Libyan government in different roles.","doc":{"id":"wiki-en-Moussa Koussa","t":"Moussa Koussa (Wikipedia)"}}],"open_question":"University staff records, or accounts from former students or colleagues, could show when and what he taught."},"u:map:tajura-camp|ounianga kebir":{"headline":"A 1983 imagery briefing places Libyan forces at Ounianga Kebir in Chad and heavy transport activity at Tajura in the same report.","explanation":"The two places appear together in one short U.S. imagery briefing on the Libya–Chad conflict from August 1983. It reports Libyan troops and guns improving defenses at Ounianga Kebir in northern Chad. It also reports a high level of heavy-lift transport activity at Tajura in Libya. The briefing does not say the Tajura activity was supplying Ounianga Kebir. The link is therefore one of shared context: Tajura was a Libyan site watched as part of the Chad campaign. Most of the document is blacked out, which limits what can be said.","strength":"weak","flag":"ok","established":["The briefing lists Ounianga Kebir, where Libyan forces were improving defenses, and Tajura, where heavy-lift transport activity continued, under one Libya–Chad heading.","At Ounianga Kebir old airfield it describes a mechanized infantry battalion, a probable tank battalion and ten 130mm M-46 field guns, six of which were deployed north of the airfield.","The Tajura activity is described as continuing at a high level, and it is the only Libya-side item."],"not_established":["The document does not state that the Tajura heavy-lift activity supported the forces at Ounianga Kebir.","It does not say what was being moved at Tajura, or where it was going.","Large parts of the text are redacted or garbled by scanning, so some detail is missing."],"context":"Libya intervened in northern Chad in the early 1980s. Tajura, east of Tripoli, hosted a military camp.","timeline":[{"when":"August 1983","what":"U.S. imagery center prepares the briefing note on Libyan activity at Ounianga Kebir, Faya Largeau and Tajura."}],"quotes":[{"text":"IN LIBYA, THE HIGH LEVEL OF HEAVY-LIFT TRANSPORT ACTIVITY CONTINUES AT TAJURA","doc":{"id":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp91t00712r000100200006-5","t":"LIBYA-CHAD CONFLICT"}},{"text":"IN CHAD, LIBYAN FORCES CONTINUE TO IMPROVE THE DEFENSES AT OUNIANGA KEBIR.","doc":{"id":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp91t00712r000100200006-5","t":"LIBYA-CHAD CONFLICT"}}],"open_question":"Fuller, unredacted imagery reports or Libyan logistics records could show whether flights or vehicles from Tajura supplied the Chad garrisons."},"u:mers el kebir naval base and shipyard|oued tatrat polisario camp no 1":{"headline":"A 1982 US imagery note judges that Libyan tanks unloaded at Mers el Kebir probably went on to a Polisario camp at Oued Tatrat.","explanation":"Both documents are copies of the same US imagery note, dated 16 September 1982. It says 16 medium tanks and three heavy trailers were newly seen at Oued Tatrat Polisario Camp No. 1 in Algeria. It judges that they \"probably\" came from Libya through the Mers el Kebir naval base and shipyard. There, 16 T-54/55 tanks and trailers had been unloaded from a Libyan cargo ship. The link is the analysts' judgment, not a confirmed record of the journey. It shows Libya's military support for the Polisario reaching beyond Tripoli. The two documents are one source, not two separate confirmations.","strength":"reported","flag":"ok","established":["The note reports 16 medium tanks and three MAZ-537 heavy-lift trailers newly identified at Oued Tatrat Polisario Camp No. 1.","It says 16 T-54/55 tanks and four MAZ-537 trailers had been offloaded at Mers el Kebir from a Libyan roll-on/roll-off cargo ship.","It says the equipment at Oued Tatrat probably arrived from Libya via Mers el Kebir.","It says the 16 tanks bring the total at the base to 43."],"not_established":["The note does not show the tanks being tracked from the dock to the camp. The link is a probable inference from the imagery.","The arrival dates and the dates of the images are blanked out in the passages, so the timing is unclear.","It does not say who in Libya ordered or organised the shipment."],"context":"Mers el Kebir is a port in Algeria near Oran. The Polisario Front was a movement in the Western Sahara dispute, and Libya backed it in the early 1980s.","timeline":[{"when":"Before 16 Sept 1982","what":"Tanks and trailers are offloaded at Mers el Kebir from a Libyan cargo ship (exact date blanked out)."},{"when":"Before 16 Sept 1982","what":"Equipment leaves the dock, except one trailer."},{"when":"16 Sept 1982","what":"The imagery note reports 16 tanks and three trailers newly identified at Oued Tatrat."}],"quotes":[{"text":"THIS EQUIPMENT PROBABLY ARRIVED FROM LIBYA, VIA MERS EL KEBIR NAVAL BASE AND SHIPYARD","doc":{"id":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp90t00784r000200360005-0","t":"PROBABLE LIBYAN ARMS DELIVERY TO POLISARIO FORCES IN ALGERIA MERS EL KEBIR NAVAL BASE AND SHIPYARD AND OUED TATRAT POLISARIO CAMP NO.1 (SANITIZED)"}},{"text":"THE RECENT ARRIVAL OF ARMOR AT A . POLISARIO CAMP IN ALGERIA, PROVIDES ADDITIONAL EVIDENCE OF CONTINUED LIBYAN SUPPORT TO THE POLISARIO.","doc":{"id":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp83t00574r000102140001-8","t":"PROBABLE LIBYAN ARMS DELIVERY TO POLISARIO FORCES IN ALGERIA MERS EL KEBIR NAVAL BASE AND SHIPYARD AND OUED TATRAT POLISARIO CAMP NO. 1 (SANITIZED)"}}],"open_question":"Later imagery, shipping records or intelligence reports on the movement from Mers el Kebir to Tindouf could confirm the route."},"u:carlos the jackal|the institute":{"headline":"A June 1979 CIA memo says Carlos was housed at \"The Institute,\" a terrorist training camp near Tripoli, and planned attacks there with Libyan intelligence.","explanation":"The memo names \"The Institute\" as a terrorist training camp near Tripoli. It says Carlos was housed there. It says he and the Libyan Intelligence Service planned operations together, and it gives the aborted 1977 attack on the US ambassador to Egypt as an example. The memo's main point is that Libya had probably ended this close relationship by 1979. It says Carlos had \"dropped out of sight,\" and other reports placed him in other countries. For Tripoli, the memo shows that a camp near the city served as a base for a well-known international terrorist, with Libyan state involvement. It is a single American intelligence assessment, and much of it is not declassified.","strength":"direct","flag":"ok","established":["The CIA memo of 1 June 1979 says Carlos was 'housed at The Institute,' a terrorist training camp near Tripoli.","It says Carlos and the Libyan Intelligence Service planned operations together, such as the aborted 1977 attack on the US ambassador to Egypt.","It says Tripoli probably decided to end its close relationship with Carlos, and that his whereabouts after 1977 were uncertain."],"not_established":["The memo gives no exact location for The Institute, only 'near Tripoli', and no dates for his stay there.","It does not say whether Carlos was still at The Institute in 1979. Some reports put him in Libya and others in Iraq, Czechoslovakia or Aden.","Part of the text is not declassified, so the memo's evidence for these claims is not shown."],"context":"Carlos, born Ilich Ramírez Sánchez, was a Venezuelan-born militant linked to the Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine (PFLP). He was widely known as 'the Jackal'.","timeline":[{"when":"1977","what":"Carlos and Libyan intelligence plan an attack on the US ambassador to Egypt, which is aborted."},{"when":"Since 1977","what":"Governments speculate about where Carlos is; reports place him in several countries."},{"when":"1 June 1979","what":"The CIA memo says Tripoli has probably ended its close relationship with him."}],"quotes":[{"text":"Housed at “The Institute,” a terrorist training camp near Tripoli, Carlos and the Libyan Intelligence Service planned such operations as the aborted attack on the US ambassador to Egypt in 1977.","doc":{"id":"frus-frus-1977-80v17p3-d100","t":"Intelligence Memorandum Prepared in the Central Intelligence Agency | Washington , June 1, 1979 | LIBYA’S CHANGING ROLE AS A PATRON STATE"}}],"open_question":"Other declassified CIA or State Department reports on Carlos in Libya could show where The Institute was and when he stayed there."}};
